Subject: netifd: find SMT siblings by core_cpus_list in packet steering

packet-steering treats CPUs that share a topology core_id as SMT
siblings and adds load to both. core_id is numbered within a cluster,
so on the K1 (two clusters of four cores, no SMT) cpu0 and cpu4, cpu1
and cpu5, and so on look like siblings although the kernel lists none.
The phantom load steers placement away from idle cores.

Identify a core by its core_cpus_list, which names the CPUs that
actually share it.

Start9 downstream patch (start-technologies projects/start-wrt).
Applies to: openwrt v25.12.6

--- a/package/network/config/netifd/files/usr/libexec/network/packet-steering.uc
+++ b/package/network/config/netifd/files/usr/libexec/network/packet-steering.uc
@@ -105,7 +105,7 @@
 cpus = map(glob("/sys/bus/cpu/devices/*"), (path) => {
 	return {
 		id: int(match(path, /.*cpu(\d+)/)[1]),
-		core: int(trim(readfile(`${path}/topology/core_id`))),
+		core: trim(readfile(`${path}/topology/core_cpus_list`)),
 		load: 0.0,
 	};
 });
